            {
              "id": "chunk_005",
              "text": "A spinal withdrawal reflex illustrates how a circuit can organize a response. A painful stimulus activates sensory neurons whose signals enter the spinal cord. Through spinal interneurons, this input can excite motor neurons supplying flexor muscles and inhibit motor neurons supplying opposing extensor muscles. The resulting pattern helps withdraw the affected limb. In this circuit, inhibition of an opposing muscle's motor neurons contributes to movement; inhibition does not always mean that the whole behavior stops. The spinal response can begin before conscious awareness of the stimulus, while ascending pathways also carry information toward the brain. Descending signals from the brain can modify spinal activity. The reflex therefore demonstrates local circuit processing within a nervous system whose regions remain connected and influence one another."
            }
